    <statement><p>Consider the recurrence relation <m>a_n = a_{n-1} + 6a_{n-2}</m> with first two terms <m>a_0 = 5</m> and <m>a_1 = 9\text{.}</m>
<ol label="a.">
<li>
<p>Find the next two terms of the sequence (<m>a_2</m> and <m>a_3</m>):</p>
<p><m>a_2 =</m> <fillin characters="3" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><m>a_3 =</m> <fillin characters="3" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Solve the recurrence relation. That is, find a closed formula for <m>a_n\text{.}</m></p>
<p><m>a_n =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>39</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>93</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left({\frac{19}{5}}\right)\cdot 3^{n}+\left({\frac{6}{5}}\right)\mathopen{}\left(-2\right)^{n}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>The sequence is <m>5, 9, {39}, {93}, {327}, \ldots</m>.  It has closed formula <m>a_n = {\left({\frac{19}{5}}\right)\cdot 3^{n}+\left({\frac{6}{5}}\right)\mathopen{}\left(-2\right)^{n}}</m>, using the characteristic root technique.</p>
</solution>
